# Exception Handling with try / catch / finally

The `try...catch` statement marks a block of statements to try and specifies a response should an exception be thrown.

### Structure
```javascript
try {
  // Code that may throw an error
} catch (error) {
  // Handles any error thrown inside the try block
} finally {
  // Always executes regardless of whether an error occurred
}
```

### The finally Block
The `finally` block runs unconditionally—even if the try or catch block returns early. It is primarily used for cleanup tasks like releasing file handles, stopping timers, or closing network streams.

codeCard.code:
function parseUserData(jsonStr) {
  try {
    const user = JSON.parse(jsonStr);
    return user.name;
  } catch (err) {
    console.error("Invalid JSON format:", err.message);
    return "Anonymous";
  } finally {
    console.log("parseUserData completed.");
  }
}

console.log(parseUserData("{ invalid }"));

