# Throwing Errors with throw

The `throw` statement allows developers to signal custom exceptional conditions and interrupt program flow.

### Best Practice: Throw Error Objects
While JavaScript permits throwing any value (strings, numbers), always throw an instance of `Error` (or a subclass) so that stack traces and error metadata are properly captured.

```javascript
if (age < 0) {
  throw new Error("Age cannot be negative");
}
```

### Custom Error Classes
You can create specialized error types by extending the built-in `Error` class:
```javascript
class ValidationError extends Error {
  constructor(message) {
    super(message);
    this.name = "ValidationError";
  }
}
```

